WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
I hate to burst your bubble, you're not the only one with that idea.

2
00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.580
Whether this person has brains, guts, and heart,

3
00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.660
Nvidia sure has a rigorous interviewing process.

4
00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.200
They're all gone, and we survived.

5
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.100
The goal of winning is just so that you can play again.

6
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.320
Even my ideas, the CEO's idea, is a bad idea.

7
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.820
You can't deliver a baby in a month with nine women.

8
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.400
We're 30 days from going out of business.

9
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:27.020
What you're about to see is Jensen Huang, the Nvidia CEO from 2003,

10
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.740
sharing his insights at Stanford University.

11
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.020
With Nvidia's tremendous success, everyone is chasing Jensen like a rock star.

12
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.980
I got really curious and started digging up all his old speeches

13
00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.300
from before he became one of the most influential entrepreneurs in the world.

14
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.200
This is a video you want to watch till the end because 21 years ago,

15
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.360
he perfectly predicted what our technology and lives would look like in 2024.

16
00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:53.200
Get ready to have your mind blown.

17
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.380
I enjoy talking to customers.

18
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.820
Purpose of selling our products clearly, but also hearing what's wrong with our products.

19
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.900
The thing that people will hear me go right to right away is, what are we doing wrong?

20
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.620
Obviously, by understanding what's wrong with our company and what's wrong with our product,

21
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:18.080
I can come back and fix it.

22
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.840
Those are rather engineering behavior.

23
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.640
The perpetual desire to fix things.

24
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.420
I think that that's a fine thing to do.

25
00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:34.180
Your company is constantly a fixer-upper, if you will, in terms of competition.

26
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.460
Some of the competition is rather obvious,

27
00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:43.320
but I think the most challenging thing about any company, any large company,

28
00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.600
is we don't know what we don't know.

29
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.520
That is, in fact, the most troubling of all.

30
00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.960
In terms of the obvious competitors, Intel is a competitor of ours.

31
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.520
Sony is a competitor of ours.

32
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.020
We have competition from all over the world still.

33
00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.240
And so what we need to figure out a way to do,

34
00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:02.400
simultaneously, as the irony has it,

35
00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.560
you want to simultaneously discover a larger market footprint,

36
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.300
therefore reducing your market share,

37
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.760
while increasing your market share.

38
00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:15.200
And so that constant discovery of expanding your market footprint

39
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.820
while capturing market share through competitive products

40
00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.440
is a 24-hour job, and it's fun.

41
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.120
In order to keep a company on the top,

42
00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.160
the employee should realize that there are other competitors

43
00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.880
having equally brainy engineers who are working on similar products.

44
00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.820
Now, as a CEO of a company,

45
00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.620
what other motivation strategies do you have for employees

46
00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.200
so that your company is on the top?

47
00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.480
It is true there are great engineers everywhere,

48
00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.140
but it is the company's choice to figure out

49
00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.640
and decide who to let in and who not to.

50
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.360
It is the company's choice to select among all those great engineers.

51
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.400
That choice ultimately goes into what the bucket I call culture.

52
00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.860
We are intensely selective in our company with respect to engineers.

53
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.480
We hire, we make offers.

54
00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.720
This is a statistic that I just recently learned.

55
00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.380
We make offers to one-tenth, 10% of the people we interview.

56
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.320
Out of those 10%, 95% accept our offers.

57
00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.940
And so we happen to be intensely selective

58
00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:16.500
when it comes down to the people that we bring to our company.

59
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.620
Not just alone in their intelligence,

60
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.340
but also their heart, you know, and also their courage.

61
00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:29.200
We try to figure out whether this person has brains, guts, and heart

62
00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.680
in the process of the interview.

63
00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.320
And that's why people say,

64
00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.660
gee, NVIDIA sure has a rigorous interviewing process.

65
00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:38.560
It's because we're trying to select the people that we want to work with.

66
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.840
Not everybody likes working at NVIDIA.

67
00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.180
Not everybody gets to come.

68
00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.000
Not everybody has to stay.

69
00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:47.500
But the people that do love working there

70
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:50.040
because we give them terrific projects,

71
00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.800
we give them extraordinary resources,

72
00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:55.280
and we also give them teammates that they know they can count on.

73
00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.540
That, I think, falls into the realm of culture.

74
00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.260
And that sets us apart.

75
00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.420
The reason why a lot of people in the Silicon Valley

76
00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.340
thinks that we have one of the best engineering organizations in the world

77
00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:08.960
is because of that, that self-selection process

78
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.240
that makes it possible for us to retain quality.

79
00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:16.480
You talk a little more about how you convey your vision to your employees

80
00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:19.380
and how you keep that sense of urgency in them

81
00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.000
so that they continue improving themselves.

82
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.900
In terms of how do we communicate a sense of urgency,

83
00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:25.420
just through action.

84
00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.300
They have to see that when I make decisions

85
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.200
or when I do something,

86
00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.360
that I do it with a sense of urgency.

87
00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.680
And it's amazing what that does.

88
00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:39.320
People simply pick up those habits from you.

89
00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:41.720
If your CEO works hard, you'll work hard.

90
00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.040
If your CEO cares, you'll care.

91
00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.320
If your CEO loves this company, you'll love this company.

92
00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.700
If your CEO is passionate about the work that we do,

93
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.500
you'll be passionate about the work that we do.

94
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.440
If your CEO does everything with an extraordinary sense of purpose

95
00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.920
and intensity and sense of urgency, you will too.

96
00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:03.060
The behavior and the values and the habits of that leader

97
00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.820
has an amazing way of rubbing off on everybody else.

98
00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.360
And I was just asked a question earlier.

99
00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.520
What do entrepreneurs do when you have a great idea?

100
00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.340
I hate to burst your bubble.

101
00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:13.800
You're not the only one with that idea.

102
00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:16.140
It is very likely that if it's a great idea,

103
00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:18.360
a lot of people have that same idea.

104
00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.920
And in fact, when NVIDIA first started as a company,

105
00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.680
we were the first consumer 3D graphics company started in the world.

106
00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:26.900
And we started the company in Silicon Valley.

107
00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:29.100
Within about 60 days,

108
00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:32.200
and certainly we didn't go out telling everybody when we started this company,

109
00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:35.000
within 60 days or so, there were 50 other companies doing it.

110
00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:36.640
Today, they're all gone.

111
00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.120
And we survived.

112
00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:43.560
But nonetheless, a lot of great people come up with great ideas at about the same time.

113
00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:47.240
The question has to do with our business historically.

114
00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:53.360
We are so incredibly well known for one thing that we do, making the GeForce GPU.

115
00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:59.580
We are known in high schools and video game parlors and in every country all over the world.

116
00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.540
I get fan mails almost every country I go to.

117
00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.560
And I'm particularly popular with the teenagers, it appears.

118
00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.140
And so we need to be careful not to reposition our company so drastically

119
00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:20.540
that we dilute what we mean to a core audience that cares deeply about us.

120
00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.600
Okay, so it's a balancing act that is, in fact, rather hard to do.

121
00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:28.500
Intel's business today is not predominantly CPUs anymore.

122
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.520
It's a very important part of their business.

123
00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.480
It is the predominant part of their profits.

124
00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:34.360
But yet they do a lot more than that.

125
00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.120
And yet we today still think of Intel as a CPU company.

126
00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:40.580
They're trying to balance that very delicately.

127
00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.760
And I need to balance that very delicately as well.

128
00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:46.280
To reposition the company into a much larger market

129
00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:51.380
while retaining the identity of our company that the whole world knows us by.

130
00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:54.960
When I come back in 10 years, if I get invited back in 10 years,

131
00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.820
I hope that I'm driving a lot more of those pixels.

132
00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:04.900
And what people see us as maybe is just a much broader consumer electronics company

133
00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:06.480
and a broader technology company

134
00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.100
and hopefully one of the most influential technology companies in the world.

135
00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:11.480
Execution certainly matters a great deal.

136
00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:18.860
And I think that within your question is, in fact, a fact

137
00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:24.460
that having simpler ideas that you can execute perfectly

138
00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:29.380
is sometimes better than having a grandiose idea that your company can't execute on.

139
00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:30.820
Okay?

140
00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.400
And so when you get large as a company and when you're trying to do complicated things,

141
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:39.540
in fact, it is best, it is most prudent to keep it simple.

142
00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.360
Many ideas surface.

143
00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.480
And a lot of people say, Jensen, why didn't you guys do this?

144
00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:45.100
Why didn't you do this?

145
00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.600
Or our engineer is asking, you know, why didn't we put this feature in?

146
00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:48.580
We could have done all that.

147
00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.700
I mean, it wasn't because we didn't have the ability to have the idea.

148
00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:58.320
You have to decide as a company, you have to decide as an engineering team or as an innovator

149
00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.300
to say, you know what, I don't need to change the world overnight.

150
00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:08.060
I'm going to change the world over the next 50 years.

151
00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:11.440
I don't need to build a killer product overnight.

152
00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:13.900
I just need to build a winning product.

153
00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:17.660
And the goal of winning is so that you can play again.

154
00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:18.980
It's just like pinball.

155
00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:24.880
If you could just play well enough to get another game, you could be there for a long time.

156
00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:30.460
With every single architecture, one of the things that we ask ourselves right off the bat is,

157
00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.460
what is the purpose of its existence?

158
00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.480
What is the soul of this product?

159
00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:38.600
I think that that's one of the things that we've done really, really terrifically in our company.

160
00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.200
Because most of our products have brand names,

161
00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:46.240
and most of these brand names are so sought after and so worldwide recognized

162
00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:50.400
that we've really given our products, these semiconductor devices, a personality.

163
00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.860
You want to create an environment where anybody can have a good idea.

164
00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:59.880
You want to create an environment where even my ideas, the CEO's idea, is a bad idea.

165
00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:09.680
And I think that you want to create an environment that is able to see adversity as an opportunity

166
00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:13.060
instead of as a life-threatening event.

167
00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:16.300
And I think if you could figure out a way to do that

168
00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:19.820
and encourage people to take calculated risks and often.

169
00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:24.680
When you take calculated risks often, you will create mistakes often.

170
00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:28.300
And when you see those mistakes as opportunity, you will get a lot of ideas often.

171
00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:29.500
I think that's just the bottom line.

172
00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:37.640
So the question has to do with the dichotomy between re-evaluation, being self-critical,

173
00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:45.440
constantly looking at the market and adjusting yourself as needed,

174
00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:49.560
versus building something of extraordinary complexity that takes two years to do.

175
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.020
In order to build anything meaningful, you have to be committed to it.

176
00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:56.740
Two years ago when we started building GeForceFX,

177
00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:00.800
the idea of building GeForceFX was completely, utterly nonsense.

178
00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:02.980
Nobody asked us to build it.

179
00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:04.940
Not a single customer said they wanted it.

180
00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:12.360
And yet you need to have the courage to go forward with executing what you,

181
00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.280
and doing what you believe is something that you're excited about.

182
00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:21.760
I mean, there's a little bit of, you know, self-entertaining that goes with building any company.

183
00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:23.760
I won't deny that.

184
00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:27.280
You know, I build GeForceFX first for me, and then second for my customers.

185
00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:29.060
And I hope that they buy some.

186
00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:33.960
So you need to have a passion, you need to have a vision, and you need to have some courage to go forward with it.

187
00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.380
Once you commit yourself to that, unless it's something pretty dramatic,

188
00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:41.880
and you don't commit yourself to a $300 million program lightly, okay?

189
00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:43.820
You really have to believe in it.

190
00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:49.480
Once you commit to it, it's very unlikely that I see something that's going to cause me to veer away from it, okay?

191
00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:55.000
And so I think that it's no different than your hobbies or, you know, something that you care deeply about.

192
00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:59.780
Even though everybody tells you it's the wrong thing to do, you are absolutely convinced of it.

193
00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:01.480
You've got to go pursue that.

194
00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:02.660
You've got to go after that vision.

195
00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:09.900
Your corporate culture is probably the single most important thing as a CEO or as an entrepreneur you ought to invest your time in.

196
00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:13.680
And that corporate culture, in our case, is about intellectual honesty.

197
00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:18.600
Intellectual honesty to us means that we have the ability to be self-critical,

198
00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.420
that we have the ability to recognize we've made mistakes,

199
00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:27.520
that we have the ability to recognize that, in fact, unless we do something, we're going to be at a competitive disadvantage.

200
00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:33.780
Okay, so those kind of intellectual honesty is really relevant, I think, in the building of companies today.

201
00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:40.180
And recognizing that as a leader in this company, that your employees have the ability to take the criticism,

202
00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:44.960
that they have the confidence to know that they're not on top of the world and that they have constant threat.

203
00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:47.660
That attitude, I think, is pretty important.

204
00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.280
A search for a fundamental advantage.

205
00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:51.600
And this is what everybody does.

206
00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:54.660
Every company is constantly looking for it.

207
00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:56.240
Every manager is asking you for it.

208
00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:59.680
I think the interesting thing is that, in fact, there are no fundamental advantages,

209
00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:06.240
unless you have a monopoly or unless you somehow carved out an airwave that nobody else can get to.

210
00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:12.300
By and large, the majority of large markets are very supportive of competition.

211
00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:17.620
And the majority of your customers, and the larger you become and the more relevant you become,

212
00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:20.220
are going to want to encourage more competition.

213
00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:23.380
And so there are no fundamental competitive advantages.

214
00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:28.720
So the question for you, then, is how do you create ongoing advantages in your company?

215
00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:30.340
You think you've got great engineers?

216
00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:31.960
Everybody's got great engineers.

217
00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:35.460
You know, Stanford University pumps out a great deal of very high-caliber engineers.

218
00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:36.480
But so does MIT.

219
00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:38.100
And so does Carnegie Mellon.

220
00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:40.740
And so does USC.

221
00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.660
Many, many universities pump out great engineers.

222
00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:46.300
And today, the great engineers all have great tools.

223
00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:48.840
And it's very likely they all have pretty good funding.

224
00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:53.180
And so the challenge, then, is now your idea is not unique.

225
00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:56.460
You also haven't discovered some kind of a fundamental secret sauce.

226
00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:01.300
And you know that your team, although extraordinary, and you're really proud of them,

227
00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:04.120
is, by and large, no better than anybody else's team.

228
00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:05.740
Everybody's got a salary cap.

229
00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:07.200
Everybody's got the same number of it.

230
00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:09.840
You know, how many engineers does it take to build a chip?

231
00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:13.260
And can you add 10,000 engineers to build a single chip?

232
00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:18.620
So it's along the lines of how many, what is the phrase of saying, you know,

233
00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:23.200
you can't deliver a baby in a month with nine women.

234
00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:26.800
I mean, I think the same thing applies with engineering.

235
00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:30.800
At some level, having 10 times more engineers is not helpful.

236
00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:36.340
What that really says is, you know, in the 10 years that I've been at NVIDIA,

237
00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:41.960
there's not been a single thing that I can currently put my finger on

238
00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:45.800
that can tell you is the sustainable idea since our beginning.

239
00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:46.540
Not one.

240
00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:49.720
Every single good idea we invented lasts 30 days.

241
00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.160
Our employees will say, Jensen's always telling us,

242
00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:53.620
we're 30 days from going out of business.

243
00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:55.640
We have a billion dollars in the bank today.

244
00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:57.760
We're not really going to go out of business in 30 days.

245
00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:03.880
The idea, the notion that you have to reinvent yourself every 30 days is a very important one.

246
00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:07.560
The idea that the world changes so rapidly.

247
00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:11.520
So whatever techniques and whatever advantages or technology you had,

248
00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:16.500
whatever strategies you had, are really, in fact, going to be obsolete 30 days from now.

249
00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:18.460
And so you have to stay on your toes.

250
00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:19.760
You have to innovate constantly.

251
00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:22.720
And you have to assume that you're going out of business every 30 days.

252
00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:24.660
There are, in fact, interesting technologies.

253
00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:33.940
But one of the interesting technologies is extremely lightweight displays that you put on as goggles with head tracking.

254
00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:39.140
And so all of a sudden, you are immersed in a 3D environment,

255
00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:45.440
not by moving joysticks or the mouse or, you know, keyboards or whatever it is,

256
00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:46.800
just by looking around.

257
00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:52.540
That has, in fact, quite an amazing ability to suspend you in disbelief.

258
00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:55.120
The technology that exists today is rather expensive.

259
00:14:55.980 --> 00:15:00.060
The lightweight ones with high resolution is extremely expensive, but it does exist today.

260
00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:04.420
There's no question that this type of technology will be available in the future.

261
00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:05.840
The question is what's beyond that.

262
00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:12.520
You know, I'll tell you that I don't know, but I'm certain that as I get closer, I'll discover it.

263
00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:19.060
In terms of engineers around the world, it is true that India and China, particularly those two countries,

264
00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:25.620
generate and produce some of the finest students in the world.

265
00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:31.280
Culturally, those two countries take education extremely, extremely seriously.

266
00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:33.960
I would say that China even more so.

267
00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:47.040
I happen to be a bit more enthusiastic about our long-term viability because I do believe that this is not an industry that is particularly scalable,

268
00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:54.000
meaning that having 1,000 engineers is not necessarily the most important thing to have.

269
00:15:54.420 --> 00:16:00.000
Having the right engineers, having the right culture, being close to the events, unfortunately.

270
00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:07.820
Being near Silicon Valley, being close to a spirit of innovation, I think is rather important.

271
00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:15.280
That's not to say that there aren't some parts of our engineering work that can't be outsourced to the international arena.

272
00:16:16.660 --> 00:16:20.520
In our particular case, in the case of NVIDIA, we just haven't learned how to do it yet.

273
00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:21.920
Where are we going to go from here?

274
00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:29.060
When we first started the company about 10 years ago, the majority of us see pixels, see graphics from a CRT.

275
00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:31.400
All of the computers at NVIDIA were CRT-based.

276
00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:34.280
All of the computers in our demo room were CRT-based.

277
00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:40.860
CRT-based computing fundamentally is challenged from a transportability perspective,

278
00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:45.940
it's challenged from a miniaturization perspective, and it's challenged from a cost perspective.

279
00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:50.680
It will never, ever be light, it will never be cheap, it will never be everywhere.

280
00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:56.820
And yet, all of a sudden, out of nowhere, LCD technology, powered by semiconductor technology, is now everywhere.

281
00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:06.780
The LCD technology, and of course in the future, other type of technologies, as OLEDs, will make displays more and more prevalent.

282
00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:12.340
And the last thing that I learned is that the IPO is in fact not guaranteed.

283
00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:20.280
At the time that you came up with the idea, the road between that idea to going public is such a long road,

284
00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:25.060
that if that is the reason why you're doing it, you're going to have a very hard time.

285
00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:31.060
And for a lot of people that start companies, or even companies that go public and then subsequently went out of business,

286
00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:37.640
if that is the purpose for you doing it, I think that you're going to find that you're going to be heartbroken many times along the way.

287
00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:40.360
And you're going to break the hearts of a lot of people who have trusted you,

288
00:17:40.820 --> 00:17:42.780
and break the hearts of a lot of people who have followed you.

289
00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:44.480
So you have to do it for the right reason.

290
00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:51.000
But certainly my right reason, the reason for our company, has been to build a company that is sustainable,

291
00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:55.580
build a company that is influential, build a company that is respected.

292
00:17:56.540 --> 00:18:00.480
And ultimately, for us, what it was all about was building a company that made a difference.

293
00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:06.220
And so, you know, for us, that was our reason, and that was our reason that's good enough any day.

294
00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:10.420
Whether you're about to go out of business, that's a great reason.

295
00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:16.080
Whether you're on top of the world and your stock is trading at whatever it is, that's a great reason.

296
00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:23.320
Or the next day when the investors decide that they no longer like your company, although nothing fundamentally changed,

297
00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:24.780
that's also a great reason.

298
00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:29.760
So you need to find yourself a reason that can survive the swings in the economy,

299
00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:33.720
the swings in the stock market, and the swings in competition.

300
00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:37.740
And so that's my last slide.
